# Global Rules

## Conventions
Working code that breaks conventions must be rejected.

## Plan Before Build
Show a Plan Block before generating code.

## Model Citation
Always cite repo paths/components used as examples.

## No Silent Deviations
Propose options if no precedent exists.

## Refuse Unknowns
If APIs, flags, or DS components are unclear, stop and ask.
- Check `util/auth/feature-flags.ts` for feature flag patterns
- Check `util/tracking/constants.ts` for analytics event naming
- Check `pattern-library` for available DS components

## Production-Only Code
No TODOs, mocks (outside unit tests), console.log, inline CSS, or temporary shims.

## After Each Prompt
Clean imports, remove unused exports/code, confirm type safety.

No stray WIP code left in the repo.

## Formatting pipeline (before commit or “done”)

**Never commit or hand off work without passing Prettier.** Lefthook pre-commit runs
`prettier --check` on **staged** `*.{ts,js,md,yml}` files; CI runs `npm run format`
on the **whole repo**. Agents often fail on `.md` / `.cursor/**` files they edited.

**Required sequence (run in the repo root):**

```bash
npm run format-fix
npm run format
npm run lint
npm run ts-build
```

- **`format-fix` first** — do not skip straight to `format` (check-only) and commit.
- Re-stage any files Prettier rewrote, then commit.
- If the pre-commit hook fails on format: run `format-fix`, re-stage, create a **new**
  commit (do not amend unless user rules allow).

See `.cursor/templates/task_template.md` §8 “Pre-commit pipeline” for full detail.

## Follow-up Prompts
All corrections, edits, or refinements must also follow these rules.
